# 🌺 English Contemplation

## **Experience Changes—Does Consciousness?**

A profound statement was offered by Muralidhar Acharya Ji:

**“As one’s inner orientation is, so the corresponding realization appears.”**

This becomes especially illuminating beside the Yoga Vāsiṣṭha teaching that Consciousness knows both bondage and liberation yet does not itself undergo change.

Our perceptions are conditioned.

Fear colors a world.

Attachment colors a relationship.

Body-identification colors birth, aging, pleasure and death with the conviction: **“This is happening to me.”**

A different understanding can transform the entire meaning of the same experience.

Yet Advaita asks one question deeper:

### **What knows both interpretations?**

Thought changes.

Belief changes.

Emotion changes.

The sense of bondage changes.

Even the recognition called liberation appears as something known.

Therefore the changing content of experience cannot, by itself, define the nature of the knowing light.

Spiritual practice certainly refines the mind. Right orientation matters. Satsanga matters. Contemplation matters.

But none of them manufactures Consciousness.

They remove confusion about what has always been present.

Thus:

### **Bhāvanā can color experience.

It cannot color the light by which bhāvanā itself is known.**

Liberation, therefore, is not the transformation of Consciousness from a bound state into a liberated state.

### **It is the ending of the mistaken attribution of bondage to that which was never bound.**

